{#
    `.. versionadded::`, `.. versionchanged::`, `.. deprecated::`.

    **This is a note, and not nearly as loosely as it sounds.** `sds-note` says
    of itself: a title that states the fact, and a bounded line of prose under
    it saying what the fact costs the reader. A version change is that sentence
    twice over — "Changed in version 1.2" is the fact, and the paragraph under
    it is what the change costs somebody reading the page today. Nothing new
    has to be invented to say it, and a vocabulary that grew a second
    label-over-prose block would have two of them disagreeing within a release.

    **Only deprecation carries a tone.** `warn` in this system is the degraded
    but usable answer, which is exactly what a deprecated thing is: it still
    works, and it is going away. The other two are facts about the surface and
    nothing has gone wrong in either, so they are `info` — and `info` is the
    tone that does not tint. That matters on a reference page, where an API
    with a long history carries one of these every third paragraph: tint them
    all and the page reads as an alarm about itself, which is the reasoning
    `sds-note` already states about `warn` being the only filled tone.

    The label the glyph says out loud stays the tone's own. The heading is
    visible and already names the version, so a truer word here would only be
    read out twice.
#}
{%- set tone = node.type == 'deprecated' ? 'warn' : 'info' -%}
{#- `versionLabel` arrives formatted — the node has already put the version
    into "New in version %s". It is text, which is what a heading takes. -#}
<sds-note tone="{{ tone }}" heading="{{ node.versionLabel }}">{{ renderNode(node.value) }}</sds-note>
